WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.760
Cloud visibility depends on logs and telemetry. In a cloud environment,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.020
many important actions happen through APIs and automation.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:13.120
Resources can be created, changed, exposed, tagged, moved,

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:16.140
or deleted without anyone logging into a server.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.440
If logging is missing or too short-lived,

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.740
teams may not be able to answer basic questions during review or response.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.080
Useful cloud logs come from several layers. Control-plane logs record management actions

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.300
such as creating resources,

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:35.160
changing policies, modifying network rules, or disabling a service setting.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:40.660
Identity logs show sign-ins, token use, role assumptions, failed authentication,

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.720
and changes to users, groups, roles, or service accounts.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.540
Network logs show connection patterns and rejected traffic. Storage access logs can show

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:51.720
reads,

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.460
writes, deletes, and unusual access volume.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.480
Application logs and workload telemetry explain what the system did after traffic reached

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:01.900
the application.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.760
Good logs help answer who did what, from where, to which resource, and when.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:11.140
Those questions are valuable for troubleshooting, compliance, incident response,

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.520
and ordinary engineering review.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.380
They also support accountability. A team cannot improve a baseline if it cannot see when

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.040
the baseline changes.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:25.140
Monitoring should focus on meaningful risk. Useful signals include unusual identity

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.320
activity,

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.140
new public exposure, broad policy changes, disabled logging,

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.280
unexpected data access spikes, repeated failed login patterns,

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.820
suspicious resource creation, and configuration drift from a known baseline.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.520
Not every event needs an alert. Too much noise teaches teams to ignore the system.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:48.540
The best detection rules are tied to decisions: investigate, notify an owner,

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:52.760
open a ticket, roll back a change, or start an incident process.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.540
Logs must be enabled, retained,

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:00.720
and protected. A log that can be changed by the same identity being monitored is weaker

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:02.220
than it looks.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:06.440
Security-relevant logs should have controlled access, appropriate retention,

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.660
and protection from casual deletion.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.960
Retention should balance investigation needs, privacy, cost,

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:18.460
and policy requirements. Teams should test whether they can retrieve and understand the

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.520
logs before an urgent event.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.380
Detection is not only a tool. It is a workflow.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.320
Someone must own alerts, tune them, review them, document findings,

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:31.500
and improve controls based on what the logs reveal.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:35.880
Cloud security becomes stronger when visibility is treated as part of the system design

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.220
from the beginning,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.360
not added after something goes wrong.
